<section id="overview">
<h2>1. What Aether Launcher is</h2>
<p><strong>Aether Launcher</strong> is a Minecraft client written in <strong>Rust</strong>, a systems programming language that compiles to native machine code. The project’s goal is to remove the overhead of running the game on the JVM and to deliver up to 100× the performance of a Java-based baseline in best-case workloads such as world loading and chunk streaming.</p>
<p>“Up to” matters. It names the ceiling observed in the most favourable test, not the typical gain. MineNews applies one rule to every performance figure in this article, Aether’s included: report the hardware, game version and render distance, and say whether anyone outside the project reproduced the result.</p>
</section>

<section id="architecture">
<h2>2. Why Rust: the technical case</h2>
<p>These points describe general properties of native Rust clients, not internal details of Aether Launcher.</p>
<h3>No JVM and no garbage-collection pauses</h3>
<p>Java Edition runs inside a managed runtime. The garbage collector (GC) periodically stops or slows application threads to reclaim memory, which shows up as frame-time spikes and stutter during chunk loading. Rust has no tracing GC. It manages memory through <em>ownership</em> and <em>borrowing</em>, checked at compile time, so allocations are released deterministically.</p>
<h3>No JIT warm-up</h3>
<p>The JVM starts by interpreting bytecode and compiles hot paths just in time. Early gameplay therefore runs slower than steady state. A Rust binary is compiled ahead of time and runs optimised machine code from the first frame, which helps startup and the first seconds of world load.</p>
<h3>Control of memory layout</h3>
<p>Rust lets developers choose data-oriented layouts, such as contiguous arrays of block states per chunk section, which are friendly to CPU caches and SIMD vectorisation. Fewer cache misses translate directly into faster meshing, lighting and entity updates.</p>
<h3>Safe concurrency</h3>
<p>Rust’s type system rejects data races at compile time. That makes it practical to move chunk generation, lighting and mesh building onto worker threads and keep the render thread free, a common design in native engines.</p>
<h3>Lower memory footprint</h3>
<p>Without a managed heap, object headers or heap-sizing slack, resident memory can be substantially lower, which matters on laptops and integrated graphics.</p>
</section>

