<section id="analysis">
<h2>4. How to read “up to 100×”</h2>
<p><em>This section is MineNews analysis by Jim Deadman.</em></p>
<p>“Up to” names the best result in the most favourable test. The sample data above peaks at 6.5× for world load time, with memory roughly halved, and shows that gains are uneven: a lower peak frame rate at 32 chunks, and slower post-teleport chunk loading on the older laptop.</p>
<p>Three conclusions follow. Removing the JVM pays off most in load time, memory and frame-time consistency, where GC pauses and warm-up are costly. Steady-state frame rate depends on the renderer and GPU driver at least as much as on the language. A 100× figure should be read as an upper bound for a narrowly defined workload and published with full methodology.</p>
</section>

<section id="glossary">
<h2>5. Glossary</h2>
<dl class="gl">
<dt>JVM (Java Virtual Machine)</dt><dd>The managed runtime that executes Java bytecode, including Minecraft: Java Edition.</dd>
<dt>Garbage collection (GC)</dt><dd>Automatic memory reclamation in managed runtimes. Collection pauses can cause stutter.</dd>
<dt>JIT (just-in-time) compilation</dt><dd>Compiling frequently executed bytecode to machine code at run time, which causes a warm-up period.</dd>
<dt>Native binary</dt><dd>A program compiled ahead of time to machine code for a specific CPU architecture and operating system.</dd>
<dt>Chunk</dt><dd>A 16×16-column section of the Minecraft world that is generated, lit, meshed and rendered as a unit.</dd>
<dt>Render distance</dt><dd>How many chunks around the player are loaded and drawn. Higher values multiply load and memory cost.</dd>
<dt>Frame time</dt><dd>Milliseconds to produce one frame. Consistency of frame time determines how smooth the game feels, beyond average fps.</dd>
<dt>Differential harness</dt><dd>A test that runs the same input through a reimplementation and a reference and compares the results.</dd>
</dl>
</section>

